The vulnerability is a **Use-of-uninitialized-value** in `parseLargeCodeblock`. Let me look at the `HighPassBand::decode` function more carefully:

```cpp
void VC5Decompressor::Wavelet::HighPassBand::decode(const Wavelet& wavelet) {
  auto dequantize = [quant = quant](int16_t val) -> int16_t {
    return mVC5DecompandingTable[uint16_t(val)] * quant;
  };

  data = Array2DRef<int16_t>::create(wavelet.width, wavelet.height);
  const Array2DRef<int16_t> dst(data.data(), wavelet.width, wavelet.height);

  BitPumpMSB bits(bs);
  // decode highpass band
  int pixelValue = 0;
  unsigned int count = 0;
  int nPixels = wavelet.width * wavelet.height;
  for (int iPixel = 0; iPixel < nPixels;) {
    getRLV(&bits, &pixelValue, &count);
    for (; count > 0; --count) {
      if (iPixel > nPixels)
        ThrowRDE("Buffer overflow");
      data[iPixel] = dequantize(pixelValue);
      ++iPixel;
    }
  }
  ...
}
```

The issue: if `count` is 0 from `getRLV`, the inner loop doesn't execute, and `iPixel` doesn't advance. This could cause an infinite loop, but more importantly, if the RLV decoding produces a `count` that doesn't fill all pixels, some `data[iPixel]` entries remain uninitialized. Then when `checkMemIsInitialized()` is called, MSAN would flag it.

Actually, looking more carefully - the loop is `for (int iPixel = 0; iPixel < nPixels;)`. If `getRLV` returns `count=0`, the inner loop doesn't run and `iPixel` stays the same, causing an infinite loop. But if the total count from all RLV codes is less than `nPixels`, the loop would exit with some pixels uninitialized... wait no, the loop only exits when `iPixel >= nPixels`. If the total count is less than nPixels, the loop would keep calling getRLV until it throws or the bits run out.

